- op: update  # 1e27
  at: '2026-09-20T03:45:03Z'  # 1e27
  by: agent  # 1e27
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1e27
  clock: 3  # 1e27
  nonce: '1e27'  # 1e27
  set:  # 1e27
    description: "RULED BY THE OWNER 2026-09-19: shape C. ADR-0057 section 15 carries the rule; this row is its implementation.\n\nTHE SITUATION, measured on hypergumbo-core with the scip-python backend (WI-nanom): the syntax arm emits a method-call stub whose receiver module it could not determine as `python:external:0-0:<name>:unresolved`. The SCIP arm emits the SAME call site as `python:<module>:0-0:<name>:unresolved` (e.g. `python:pathlib.Path:0-0:glob:unresolved`) — the syntax arm's own id shape when IT knows the module. Where the syntax arm already knew the module the ids are identical and the merge pass folds them; where it did not, the dst ids DIFFER and the artifact carries TWO edges for one call (3,973 of 4,133 sites, 96.1%). `unknown_receiver_scope` counts the module-less stub as untyped regardless of its typed twin, so the backend's value proposition is invisible to verify-claims.\n\nTHE RULING (C). Neither a new fold rule nor a section 14 sibling: rule what an external endpoint's IDENTITY is, and the fold falls out of section 11 unchanged. `Edge.dst_ref` is already the canonical source of truth for external-target identity (ADR-0037 ruling 2; ir.py's Edge.dst_ref docstring), and the legacy `dst` string is built from the same ExternalRef. The two surfaces differ in one respect: `dst_ref` can ABSTAIN and the `dst` string's module segment cannot, because make_unresolved_edge's module_hint defaults to the literal \"external\". ADR-0051's axiom classifies that literal as a sentinel — \"not a marker for the absence of an answer\" — so reading identity off the slot that cannot abstain is the absent-versus-empty defect at identity time. An external identity key whose module component is absent is PARTIAL, and a partial key matches a complete key that agrees on every component it states. Two partial keys do not match each other.\n\nWHY NOT A. A is right about the mechanism and wrong about the provenance: it kept the stub's dst \"as an alternative\", which records the sentinel as a VALUE and re-commits the defect inside the slot that exists to cure it. Under section 1 an abstention contributes no entry at all, so `alternatives` gets no dst_ref entry.\n\nWHY NOT B. B asserts that the two edges are two relationships, which is the thing being refuted. It also required teaching `unknown_receiver_scope` to skip a stamped stub AND to stop double-counting its denominator — two edits to an instrument to make a demotion legible — and it would have extended section 14, whose stated scope was measured false and is now corrected in the ADR.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION SHAPE (detail in ADR-0057 section 15).\n- KEY: section 14's key verbatim — same `src`, a shared call line, the same `edge_type`, the same callee name under the incumbent's DECLARED name_key — plus \"exactly one side states a module\". Measured on this repository: 86,839 distinct site groups over 63,329 external `calls` edges, 14 hold more than one edge, 10 of those mix a sentinel module with a real one. Those 10 are the hazard and section 15.5 REFUSES them onto MergeReport.ambiguous rather than guessing.\n- PLACEMENT: `analyze/merge_producers.py::_fold_edges`, a second phase over the leftovers of the existing `(src, dst, edge_type)` grouping. It must be BEFORE `deduplicate_edges`, which collapses N call sites into one edge carrying meta[\"call_lines\"]: 12,126 external call edges on this repository are post-collapse multi-site, 4,818 of them sentinel-module, so a fold placed after dedup would assert the typed module of every site the stub collapsed.\n- SURVIVOR: incumbent-first, `origin` unioned, the STATED dst_ref, the `dst` string rebuilt by format_legacy_dst, `Edge.id` re-minted (Edge.create derives it from dst), `edge_key` reset. `confidence` combines under section 13 — the pathways are distinct, so the survivor is corroborated.\n- NO NEW PRODUCER DECLARATION, and none is permitted: MergeAnchor.observes exists only for attributes whose VALUE cannot express abstention, and `dst_ref` can. A producer states per edge whether it determined the module.\n- CONSUMERS: `unknown_receiver_scope` needs no edit — the stub is gone and the survivor carries a real module, so the site leaves the numerator and stays in the denominator once. That the metric does not have to learn anything is the test the placement passes.\n\nALSO CLOSED BY THE FOLD, and not otherwise: `unknown_receiver_scope`'s denominator currently counts BOTH edges of every doubled call, because scip-python's external edges carry call_construct, is_resolved=False and language python. Under WI-nanom the caveat reads \"4,133 of ~8,266\" — understating the untyped share by half while still firing on every site.\n\nBLOCKED BY INV-difud (one callee-name reader): the fold needs a chain reading meta[\"callee_name\"] AND dst_ref.name, because the scip-python arm stamps no callee_name and the syntax arm stamps no dst_ref.\n\nKEY HAZARD TO PIN BEFORE ANY RUST LANDING: double-span external ids (`rust:external:0-0:Stdio:0-0:null:external_symbol`) read differently under symbol_name_slot and a positional parse. INV-difud's chain settles which reader wins."  # 1e27
